// Every robot of a stack as one MCP surface: who each robot is, its posture,
// arm and gripper moves and the stop that ends them, its limbs' live state,
// its collision readout, its cameras with their frames, controls, geometry
// and mounts, where its design lets it work, its brain and its recorder,
// each addressed by the robot's name on one endpoint, and the record of the
// calls that changed a robot's state.
//
// The document is written against contracts, never against a model. Each
// target below is one contract a robot may bring, and the listing tool
// names the tools and the resources a robot's targets give it. The same
// document is served on the physical fleet and in every simulation, so
// everything a model does here transfers between them.
//
// `robots` makes it a per-robot surface: every target is a set the stack's
// robots fill, a join adding its robot's instances and a removal taking
// them out while the server runs, and every tool takes `robot`, the name
// robot.list reports. A target with an `argument` is filled any number of
// times per robot, once per camera, and a call on it names the camera too.
//
// A launcher deploys it once per stack under
// `source: { exposures: ["robot_control:v1"] }` with no `links`: the
// fragments a robot's copy runs add its instances to the targets with
// `add_links`, the launchers hub's `robot_control` option. The server built into
// peppy serves it at /robot_control/v1/mcp.
{
  peppy_schema: "mcp_exposure/v1",
  manifest: { name: "robot_control", tag: "v1" },

  // Advertised to clients through `server/discover`. `instructions` is the
  // prose a model reads before it decides what to call: what every robot
  // shares. What differs between robots is what robot.list answers.
  server: {
    title: "Robots",
    instructions: "Every robot of the stack, drivable by name on this one endpoint, whatever its model. Call robot.list first: for each robot present it reports the tools that robot answers under tools, including its brain's and its recorder's where it has them; the resources it publishes under resources, each read at peppy://resource/<name>; its camera names under members; its model and the machine hosting it under identity; and its arm and gripper names, with the joints behind each arm, under limbs. Every other tool takes robot, one of the names robot.list reports, and a camera tool takes camera too, one of the names the listing gives that robot under members. A call naming a robot that is not there, a tool that robot does not answer, or a camera it does not have is refused, and the refusal names what is there. This is the robots' own surface: what a robot cannot do through these tools, it cannot do, so prefer it over any simulation endpoint, and when the robots run in a simulation their names here are their names in that endpoint's scene.get_robots_list. Every limb tool takes the arm and gripper names the listing reports under limbs. Distances are meters, durations seconds, angles radians. A pose is the gripper's grasp point, midway between the finger pads, in the robot frame: position [x, y, z] and orientation as a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]. The robot frame is fixed to the robot's base: its origin is the point the base stands on, +X points the way the robot faces, +Y to its left and +Z up; it moves with the robot and is not a frame of the room or of a simulated world. In a simulation, scene.get_robots_list on the simulated world's endpoint reports where this frame stands in the world. Look before moving: call camera.look for the picture a color camera sees and depth_camera.look for the color picture of a camera that carries depth, and call depth_camera.look_depth to see how far things are, a grayscale picture where white is near and black is far. To compute with the measurements, read the resource depth_camera.latest_depth_samples, a 16-bit PNG in the unit its depth_info reports. Call robot.move_to_ready before any robot.move_arm; the rest posture is not a place to plan Cartesian moves from. Call robot.check_arm_move to learn whether a pose has a plan before moving to it. One motion per limb at a time: a goal for a limb that is still moving is refused. No robot move checks the floor, a table, an object or an item a gripper holds: a robot with a collision guard, such as openarm_v2, keeps only its own arms, grippers and body apart, and a robot without one, such as so101, checks no collision at all. A move that reports success true does not prove that the arm arrived: an arm can stop short of its goal, against an obstacle or where a robot's collision guard holds it, so compare the pose the move's result reports with the goal. To stop a robot, call robot.stop; brain.abort ends a brain sequence between its moves. To turn a pixel of a camera's picture into a point of the robot frame, read its depth from depth_camera.latest_depth_samples, deproject it with camera_geometry.color_intrinsics into the camera's optical frame, and carry it into the robot frame with the pose robot.get_camera_poses reports for that camera. When you find a robot in a state you did not command, call robot.recent_calls: it lists the last state-changing calls of this endpoint with the client that made each, so you can tell whether another client did it. Keep requested durations short: each move's deadline is 60 s (30 s for robot.move_gripper), and a duration_s of 0 means as fast as the joint limits allow. Each robot refuses outright a posture duration above its own limit. On a robot with a brain, brain.grab_item, brain.place_item and brain.drop_item run whole sequences on its arms through the same backbone the direct moves use; prefer them when the robot has a brain and use the direct moves for a single deliberate motion, since a backbone refuses a second motion on a limb that is moving whichever surface asked. Call robot.describe_workspace to learn where to put items for a task, and robot.check_positions before the robot grabs an item that brain.scan_items found. The camera setters drive the device's controls in its own units and ranges, which camera_profile.get reports where the camera implements it; a control the device does not support is refused, a refused request changes nothing and says why in message, and every setter answers with the effective value after the call, so read the response rather than assuming the request took.",
  },

  // The record of the state-changing calls this endpoint takes: every call
  // of a tool that is not read-only, tasks included, the last 200 of them.
  call_record: {
    tool: "robot.recent_calls",
    description: "List the last calls of this endpoint that could change a robot's state, newest first, up to 200: every mutating tool and every action, with started_at, the client that made the call (its declared name and version, or null), the tool, its arguments as sent (null when larger than 1 kB, with arguments_bytes), outcome (running, completed, failed, cancelled or refused), success, message and duration_ms. Read outcome and success together: a completed call with success false ran to its end and reports a failure. The record holds the calls of MCP clients on this endpoint only; a motion a teleoperation or another node commanded is not in it, so when no entry accounts for a state, no client of this endpoint caused it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    keep: 200,
  },

  robots: {
    list: {
      tool: "robot.list",
      description: "List every robot of the stack. Each entry carries robot, its name, which every other tool takes; tools, the tools that robot answers, each called with its name; resources, the resources it publishes, each read at peppy://resource/<name>; members, the camera names its camera tools take, under camera for color cameras and depth_camera for cameras that carry depth; identity, its model and the machine hosting it; limbs, its arm and gripper names with the joints behind each arm; and notes, anything that could not be read for it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    },
    describe: {
      identity: { target: "identity", service: "get_identity" },
      limbs: { target: "limb_state", service: "get_limb_names" },
    },
  },

  targets: {
    // Who each robot is: its initializer answers, the node that works the
    // name out and joins a simulation under it, so the name reported here
    // is the one a simulation lists the robot under.
    identity: {
      contract: {
        name: "robot_identity",
        tag: "v1",
        // An author pin: the sha256 of the exact contract document this file
        // was written against (`sha256sum robot/robot_identity.json5` in
        // the contracts hub). A launch that resolves other bytes refuses,
        // naming the contract. Every pin below is the same kind.
        sha256: "ebdd513c16af87a3ef326ba57f0e88cb5be2890a1634e23329621c159cbe9983",
      },
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_identity",
          tool: "robot.get_identity",
          description: "Report who the robot is: robot is the name it stands under everywhere, model its model id, for example openarm_v2 or so101, and core_node the machine hosting it. robot.list reports the same under identity. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // Whole-robot moves: the backbone's. An action becomes a tool. For a
    // client that declares the MCP tasks extension the call returns a task
    // handle, `tasks/get` reports its progress and `tasks/cancel` forwards
    // to the action's cancel path; for any other client the goal runs
    // inside the call, which answers with the terminal result. Neither
    // contract declares a feedback topic, so a move reports its state
    // transitions and the terminal result, not interim progress.
    postures: {
      contract: {
        name: "postures",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "ea11a3ebf99228cb8353da08ca9f5e39330db549bf7b7d71471f2529e07e15af",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "move_to_ready",
          tool: "robot.move_to_ready",
          description: "Bring every arm of the robot to its working posture, raised out in front of it. duration_s is the requested move time in seconds; 0 means as fast as the joint limits allow. After the call, the result gives arm_names and, in the same order, positions (3 per arm: x, y, z in meters) and orientations (4 per arm: a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]), the grasp point of each arm in the robot frame as measured when the move ended. The result gives this pose whatever success says, so after a cancel or robot.stop it is where the arms stopped. The three arrays are empty when the robot has no measured pose to report for one of its arms, and message then says why. The move sends no command to the grippers, so each gripper goes on as its last gripper command drives it.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: this one moves the robot.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_to_home",
          tool: "robot.move_to_home",
          description: "Bring every arm of the robot to its rest posture, lower and closer to the robot's body than at its working posture. duration_s is the requested move time in seconds; 0 means as fast as the joint limits allow. After the call, the result and the grippers are as robot.move_to_ready describes. Close the grippers first: on some robots, such as openarm_v2, open jaws stop against the robot's body before the arms reach the rest posture.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // One limb at a time: the backbone's. The arm and gripper names are the
    // robot's own, which robot.list reports under limbs.
    limb_motion: {
      contract: {
        name: "limb_motion",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "1f48069030e6b7081e6b2897ed66aa152bcaa833506f6e65cae8a08b7a223ef8",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "move_arm",
          tool: "robot.move_arm",
          description: "Move one arm's grasp point to a pose in the robot frame. arm_name is one of the robot's arms as robot.list reports them; position is [x, y, z] in meters and orientation a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]; duration_s 0 means as fast as the joint limits allow. plan_position_tolerance_m and plan_orientation_tolerance_rad bound how far the planned pose may sit from the requested one (0 takes the planner's defaults). After the call, when the move ran, final_position and final_orientation give where the grasp point was when the move ended, from the joints the robot measured then, or from the joints it commanded last when message says so. A pose no plan reaches inside both tolerances is refused rather than approximated: call robot.check_arm_move first to learn whether the pose has a plan.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_arm_joints",
          tool: "robot.move_arm_joints",
          description: "Move one arm to a joint configuration. arm_name is one of the robot's arms as robot.list reports them; joint_positions holds one value in radians per joint of that arm, as many as robot.list reports for it under limbs, in the order robot.limb_state reports joint_positions for that arm; duration_s 0 means as fast as the joint limits allow. After the call, final_joint_positions are the joints the robot measured when the move ended, or the joints it commanded last when message says so. A vector of another length, or a value outside the joint limits, is refused. The way to use it: read the arm's joints from robot.limb_state and pass them back later to return to that configuration.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_gripper",
          tool: "robot.move_gripper",
          description: "Drive one gripper to an opening. gripper_name is one of the robot's grippers as robot.list reports them; opening is a fraction of full jaw travel, 0 closed and 1 fully open. max_effort caps the grip in the gripper's own effort unit, 0 meaning no cap from you; it can only lower the gripper's own limit, and some grippers, such as the so101's, ignore it. After the call, the gripper keeps driving toward the opening it was sent to, so it keeps squeezing what it holds, until the next gripper move or a leader that streams to the gripper. The tool answers when the gripper stands still, and final_opening is the opening measured then. It differs from opening when an object or the effort cap holds the jaws: the move still reports success true, with a message that names both openings, so read final_opening to judge a grasp. The result reports the opening the jaws reached, not the force they press with.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
      ],
      services: [
        {
          member: "stop",
          tool: "robot.stop",
          description: "Stop the robot: end every planned move in flight on it, whoever started it, the brain's moves included. Each move ends as cancelled with a message that names the stop and reason, which may be empty. After the call, each limb holds where it was commanded to be when the stop came, and no gripper opens, so a held item stays held. It does not stop a leader that streams setpoints to the robot, such as a teleoperation. Answers with stopped, the limbs whose move it ended, empty when nothing was moving; a stop with nothing in flight succeeds. A move sent after the stop runs.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "check_arm_move",
          tool: "robot.check_arm_move",
          description: "Learn whether a robot.move_arm goal with these fields has a plan from where the arm is commanded now, without moving anything. The fields are those of robot.move_arm, with the same meaning. success true comes with duration_s, the time the move would take; success false comes with the message the move's refusal would give, also when the arm is unknown or is executing a move. The check finds a plan and no more: it does not check collisions with the robot itself or with the room, so a move that passes the check can still stop short. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // What each robot's limbs are called and where they are: the backbone
    // answers the names on demand, and streams the live snapshot as a
    // resource per robot. robot.list carries the names.
    limb_state: {
      contract: {
        name: "limb_state",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "90a66654f090cbb36ed2a7f8dc95d2461082d5371dfc8b01bc84c5b263f5fea4",
      },
      topics: [
        {
          member: "limb_states",
          resource: "robot.limb_state",
          description: "The robot's latest limb state: arm_names and gripper_names, joints_per_arm and joint_positions flattened arm by arm, each arm's grasp point in the robot frame as positions (3 per arm, meters) and orientations (4 per arm, a unit quaternion), and gripper_openings as fractions of full jaw travel, at timestamp.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 5 },
          // The arrays are variable-length, so the runtime size policy is
          // mandatory; a limb state is small, and one that is not is a
          // fault to refuse.
          max_result_bytes: 65536,
          on_oversize: "reject",
        },
      ],
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_limb_names",
          tool: "robot.get_limb_names",
          description: "Report the robot's limbs: arm_names the arm names every limb tool takes, joints_per_arm how many joints each of those arms has, and gripper_names the gripper names. robot.list reports the same under limbs. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // The proximity readout of a backbone that watches for self-collision:
    // a resource per robot that has one.
    collision: {
      contract: {
        name: "collision_status",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "f5d94310b69f2039c51b188fa842da0d4a19eb0440b66d89d548173fea0633cd",
      },
      topics: [
        {
          member: "collision_status",
          resource: "robot.collision_status",
          description: "The robot's latest collision readout: distance in meters between the closest pair of links link_a and link_b, whether the governor is throttling motion (throttled) or has stopped it (stopped), at timestamp.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 5 },
          // The link names are strings, so the runtime size policy is
          // mandatory; a readout is small, and one that is not is a fault
          // to refuse.
          max_result_bytes: 4096,
          on_oversize: "reject",
        },
      ],
    },

    // A color camera: one per camera of the robot, named by `camera` on
    // every call, as the launcher wrote its instance (wrist_left, wrist).
    // The topic becomes a resource per robot and camera, published as JPEG
    // at no more than 2 Hz however fast the camera streams, and the picture
    // tool a model looks through the camera with.
    camera: {
      contract: {
        name: "rgb_camera",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "a4e66999b7ec166569a73d25d430878823a368408e76d4aaa56e58ea89271b4f",
      },
      argument: "camera",
      topics: [
        {
          member: "video_stream",
          resource: "camera.latest_frame",
          description: "The camera's latest frame. A read gives the message without its frame, as JSON: header with the capture timestamp and frame_id, encoding, width and height; and the frame as a JPEG blob. To look through the camera, call camera.look, which answers with the same frame as a picture.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 2 },
          representation: {
            image: "jpeg",
            quality: 80,
            fields: {
              data: "frame",
              encoding: "encoding",
              width: "width",
              height: "height",
            },
          },
          // The frame is a variable-length byte array, so the runtime size
          // policy is mandatory.
          max_result_bytes: 524288,
          on_oversize: "downscale",
          // The same snapshot through a tool, whose result carries the frame
          // as an image.
          picture: {
            tool: "camera.look",
            description: "Look through one of the robot's color cameras: its latest frame as a JPEG picture, with the capture timestamp, width and height. robot is one of the names robot.list reports, and camera one of the names the listing gives that robot under members.camera. Changes nothing.",
          },
        },
      ],
      // Each service becomes a tool that answers within one request. One
      // schema serves every device, so a value outside a device's range is
      // refused by the device at call time, not by the schema;
      // camera_profile.get is where a model reads the range first.
      services: [
        {
          member: "video_stream_info",
          tool: "camera.info",
          description: "Report the camera stream's resolution, frame rate and encoding. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_exposure",
          tool: "camera.set_exposure",
          description: "Set the camera's exposure. mode is auto or manual; value is the manual exposure in the device's unit and range, which camera_profile.get reports, and is ignored in auto mode. A refused request changes nothing. Answers with current_value, the effective exposure after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_gain",
          tool: "camera.set_gain",
          description: "Set the camera's gain in the device's unit and range, which camera_profile.get reports. A refused request changes nothing. Answers with current_value, the effective gain after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_white_balance",
          tool: "camera.set_white_balance",
          description: "Set the camera's white balance. mode is auto or manual; temperature is the manual color temperature in kelvin, in the device's range, and is ignored in auto mode. A refused request changes nothing. Answers with current_temperature, the effective temperature after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
      // `set_brightness` and `set_contrast` are live on the cameras and
      // absent here: the devices have no measured response for either, so
      // a simulated twin has no device model to answer them with.
    },

    // A camera that carries depth beside its color: one per such camera of
    // the robot, named by `camera` on every call.
    // Its color frame is a resource as a color camera's is, and its depth
    // frame two more from the one contract member: a grayscale picture to
    // look at and the lossless samples to compute with. The two pictures are
    // picture tools too; the samples are data and stay a resource.
    depth_camera: {
      contract: {
        name: "rgbd_camera",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "61a2cc33ad04a82dd57e2afaf66b18c76ebfa1ca9b23f466825103e4513cef06",
      },
      argument: "camera",
      topics: [
        {
          member: "video_stream",
          resource: "depth_camera.latest_frame",
          description: "The camera's latest color frame. A read gives the message without its frame, as JSON: header with the capture timestamp and frame_id, encoding, width and height; and the frame as a JPEG blob. To look through the camera, call depth_camera.look, which answers with the same frame as a picture.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 2 },
          representation: {
            image: "jpeg",
            quality: 80,
            fields: {
              data: "frame",
              encoding: "encoding",
              width: "width",
              height: "height",
            },
          },
          max_result_bytes: 524288,
          on_oversize: "downscale",
          picture: {
            tool: "depth_camera.look",
            description: "Look through one of the robot's cameras that carry depth: its latest color frame as a JPEG picture, with the capture timestamp, width and height. robot is one of the names robot.list reports, and camera one of the names the listing gives that robot under members.depth_camera. Changes nothing.",
          },
        },
        {
          member: "depth_stream",
          resource: "depth_camera.latest_depth_picture",
          description: "The camera's latest depth frame drawn in grayscale, from the same viewpoint as depth_camera.latest_frame: white is near, black is far, the shades between are linear in distance, and black also marks a pixel the camera measured nothing at. The shading runs from 100 to 10000 sample steps of the depth_unit depth_camera.depth_info reports, so on a camera measuring in millimeters white is 0.1 m or nearer and black 10 m or farther. A read gives the message without its frame, as JSON: header with the capture timestamp, frame_id and align_mode, encoding, width and height; and the picture as a JPEG blob. To see how far things are, call depth_camera.look_depth, which answers with the same picture; read depth_camera.latest_depth_samples for the measurements themselves.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 2 },
          representation: {
            image: "jpeg",
            quality: 80,
            fields: {
              data: "frame",
              encoding: "encoding",
              width: "width",
              height: "height",
            },
            // The ends of the shading, in the frame's own sample counts.
            depth_range: { near: 100, far: 10000 },
          },
          max_result_bytes: 524288,
          on_oversize: "downscale",
          picture: {
            tool: "depth_camera.look_depth",
            description: "See how far things are from one of the robot's cameras that carry depth: its latest depth frame as a grayscale JPEG picture, from the same viewpoint as depth_camera.look. White is near, black is far, the shades between are linear in distance, and black also marks a pixel the camera measured nothing at. The shading runs from 100 to 10000 sample steps of the depth_unit depth_camera.depth_info reports, so on a camera measuring in millimeters white is 0.1 m or nearer and black 10 m or farther. robot is one of the names robot.list reports, and camera one of the names the listing gives that robot under members.depth_camera. Changes nothing.",
          },
        },
        {
          member: "depth_stream",
          resource: "depth_camera.latest_depth_samples",
          description: "The camera's latest depth frame as measured: one 16-bit sample per pixel in the unit depth_camera.depth_info reports as depth_unit (meters per sample step), 0 where the camera measured nothing, aligned to the color frame as header.align_mode says. A read gives the message without its frame, as JSON: header with the capture timestamp, frame_id and align_mode, encoding, width and height; and the samples as a 16-bit PNG blob. Read this one to compute with the measurements; call depth_camera.look_depth to see how far things are.",
          freshness: { max_age_ms: 2000 },
          update: { max_hz: 2 },
          // Lossless: a depth map is data, and downscaling keeps every
          // second sample as measured. Data is no picture for a model, so
          // this entry declares no picture tool.
          representation: {
            image: "png16",
            fields: {
              data: "frame",
              encoding: "encoding",
              width: "width",
              height: "height",
            },
          },
          max_result_bytes: 1048576,
          on_oversize: "downscale",
        },
      ],
      services: [
        {
          member: "video_stream_info",
          tool: "depth_camera.info",
          description: "Report the camera's color stream's resolution, frame rate and encoding. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "depth_stream_info",
          tool: "depth_camera.depth_info",
          description: "Report the camera's depth stream's resolution, frame rate and encoding, and depth_unit, the meters one sample step of depth_camera.latest_depth_samples stands for. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_color_exposure",
          tool: "depth_camera.set_exposure",
          description: "Set the camera's color exposure. mode is auto or manual; value is the manual exposure in the device's unit and range, which camera_profile.get reports, and is ignored in auto mode. A device that runs automatic exposure only refuses mode manual with success false. A refused request changes nothing. Answers with current_value, the effective exposure after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_color_gain",
          tool: "depth_camera.set_gain",
          description: "Set the camera's color gain in the device's unit and range, which camera_profile.get reports. A refused request changes nothing. Answers with current_value, the effective gain after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_color_white_balance",
          tool: "depth_camera.set_white_balance",
          description: "Set the camera's color white balance. mode is auto or manual; temperature is the manual color temperature in kelvin, in the device's range, and is ignored in auto mode. A refused request changes nothing. Answers with current_temperature, the effective temperature after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
      // `set_color_brightness` and `set_color_contrast` absent, as on a
      // color camera.
    },

    // What a camera's controls are and support, where the camera
    // implements the contract: the simulations' rendered cameras do.
    camera_profile: {
      contract: {
        name: "camera_profile",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "ebe24d01a9d916a91a229ca6b14c59d635902bbf9220f40898af8ee505dd2382",
      },
      argument: "camera",
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_camera_profile",
          tool: "camera_profile.get",
          description: "Report the camera's profile as JSON text in profile_json: the controls it supports, each with its unit, range and default. Read it before setting a control. success false with a message means the camera has no profile to report. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "reset_camera",
          tool: "camera_profile.reset",
          description: "Return every control of the camera to its default. success false with a message means the camera refused. Changes the camera's controls and nothing else.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // Where a camera's pixels point, where the camera implements the
    // contract: the intrinsics of its color and depth streams and the
    // transform between them.
    camera_geometry: {
      contract: {
        name: "camera_geometry",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "764d0ec0f33a4216e544547bc3b7f292fc48feee871a2e2134e61a6de0842f02",
      },
      argument: "camera",
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_color_intrinsics",
          tool: "camera_geometry.color_intrinsics",
          description: "Report the color stream's pinhole intrinsics: width and height in pixels, fx, fy, cx and cy in pixels, and the distortion_model with its distortion coefficients. success false with a message means the camera has none to report. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "get_depth_intrinsics",
          tool: "camera_geometry.depth_intrinsics",
          description: "Report the depth stream's pinhole intrinsics as camera_geometry.color_intrinsics does, with depth_model, min_depth_m and max_depth_m in meters, and align_mode, how the depth frame aligns to the color frame. success false with a message means the camera has no depth stream. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
        {
          member: "get_depth_to_color_extrinsics",
          tool: "camera_geometry.depth_to_color",
          description: "Report the transform from the depth stream's frame to the color stream's: depth_to_color_position in meters and depth_to_color_orientation as a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], with align_mode. success false with a message means the camera has no depth stream. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // Where the robot's design puts each of its cameras, where the robot
    // implements the contract: the backbone answers for the cameras it
    // carries, from the joints it measures.
    camera_mounts: {
      contract: {
        name: "camera_mounts",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "f4a43adc503290b0ef2929900484a54a4e0e055000533f906e771923a7edd0fb",
      },
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_camera_poses",
          tool: "robot.get_camera_poses",
          description: "Report where each of the robot's cameras stands in the robot frame now: camera_names in the robot's fixed order, and index-aligned with it positions (3 per camera, meters), orientations (4 per camera, a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]) and carried_by (the arm that carries the camera, or empty for one fixed to the base), computed from the joints measured at timestamp. A pose is that of the camera's color optical frame, +X to the right of the image, +Y down it, +Z along the view, so a point p that a pixel and its depth give in that frame (deproject the pixel with camera_geometry.color_intrinsics and a sample of depth_camera.latest_depth_samples) stands at R(orientation) * p + position in the robot frame, where robot.move_arm takes it. The values are the robot's design values: exact in a simulation, nominal on a physical robot, whose camera stands within the tolerance of its mount. A robot without cameras answers empty arrays; success false with a message means the robot has not measured its joints yet. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // Where each robot can work, where the robot implements the contract:
    // the backbone answers from the robot's design and the field of view of
    // the camera it finds items with, in the robot frame, and knows nothing
    // of the room.
    workspace: {
      contract: {
        name: "workspace",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "65fa754a3a191d5b887364977fe94da722d8cd8ba2c16b6da37c2843a90e0358",
      },
      services: [
        {
          member: "describe_workspace",
          tool: "robot.describe_workspace",
          description: "Learn where on a flat, level surface the robot can work, from the robot's design and the field of view of its perception camera, the one depth camera no arm carries, which it finds items with. It knows nothing of the room: it sees no surface and no obstacle, and takes the surface as an empty plane at surface_height, in meters above the robot's base point (a table top, for example). It measures points from 0 to 1.0 m ahead of the robot and up to 0.30 m to either side. A point is reachable when an arm brings its grasp point within 0.01 m of a target 0.04 m above it with the gripper pointing straight down or straight forward, in view when the perception camera sees it, and workable when it is both, or reachable alone for a robot whose view is not checked: one without a perception camera (perception_camera is then empty), or one whose perception camera has no camera geometry linked, as message then says. Answers with workable, true when the workable points cover at least 0.03 square meters; area, the workable part in square meters; rectangle, [x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max] in the robot frame in meters, the largest rectangle the robot can work: put the items for a task inside it; reach and view, the bounds of the points an arm reaches and of those the camera sees, in the same form; perception_camera, that camera's name, empty for a robot without one; and message, one line that says why. A rectangle or bound that no point fills is absent. success false with a message means the request was refused or the robot cannot read its perception camera's geometry now. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          // An answer reads the perception camera's geometry, up to 2 s per
          // read, and solves the arms' inverse kinematics over the grid.
          deadline_ms: 15000,
        },
        {
          member: "check_positions",
          tool: "robot.check_positions",
          description: "Learn whether the robot can work each of the given points, from the robot's design and the field of view of its perception camera, as robot.describe_workspace does. It knows nothing of the room: it checks no obstacle and nothing that could hide a point. positions holds 3 values per point, x, y and z in meters in the robot frame, each the target the grasp point would reach: give an item's position as brain.scan_items reports it. An empty list, one whose length is not a multiple of 3, and a coordinate more than 1000 meters from the robot's base point are refused. Answers with all_workable, whether the robot can work every point; perception_camera; and results, one per point in the request's order: position as asked; workable, reachable and in_view, as robot.describe_workspace defines them; arm, the arm that reaches it, by the name the limb tools take, empty when none does; short_by, how far in meters the closest arm's grasp point stops short of it whichever way the gripper points, 0 when an arm reaches it, and at most 0.01 with reachable false when the arms come that close but none reaches it with its gripper pointing down or forward; view, seen, outside_field, out_of_depth, or no_camera for a robot whose view is not checked; and message, one line that says why. success false with a message means the request was refused or the robot cannot read its perception camera's geometry now. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 15000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // A robot's brain, where it has one: the item scan and search, and the
    // manipulation sequences, on that robot's own arms. Each robot has its
    // own brain, so a brain tool names its robot like every other call.
    item_perception: {
      contract: {
        name: "item_perception",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "ed789491531873b32e4a392a27c2b6c62ced426a0588bf6910f92be23a79248d",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "scan_items",
          tool: "brain.scan_items",
          description: "Look at the scene through the robot's brain and report every item it can act on, minting an item_id for each. This is the robot's own answer to \"what do you see\": what it reports here is what it can identify and grab, so ask it rather than reading a camera frame, which is a picture for a person. timeout_s bounds the scan in seconds, 0 for the robot's default. Returns index-aligned arrays: item_ids, labels in the robot's own vocabulary (two items of one kind share a label and have distinct ids), positions as a flat list of 3 robot-frame coordinates in meters per item, confidences from 0 to 1, and regions, 4 entries per item (x0, y0, x1, y1 in pixels, x to the right and y down, four zeros for an item this look did not see) of the picture depth_camera.look gives for the camera named in camera, an image of image_width by image_height captured at frame_timestamp. An empty scene is a success with empty arrays. Scanning again keeps the id of an item the robot recognizes. Starts no motion.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "identify_item",
          tool: "brain.identify_item",
          description: "Find one item through the robot's brain and mint an item_id for it. This is the robot's own answer to \"is there a banana\" or \"where is the mug\": ask it rather than judging from a camera frame, since only an item it identifies here can be grabbed. description is the item's plain name, two or three words at most, such as \"red mug\" or \"banana\": no article, no sentence, no position (\"the mug on the left\" becomes \"red mug\"); empty for the most prominent item the robot can act on; timeout_s bounds the search in seconds, 0 for the robot's default. Returns item_id, a label in the robot's own vocabulary, a robot-frame position in meters, an orientation only when the robot resolved one, confidence from 0 to 1, and region, the item's x0, y0, x1, y1 in pixels of the picture depth_camera.look gives for the camera named in camera, an image of image_width by image_height captured at frame_timestamp. Finding nothing is a failure whose message says so. Starts no motion.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
      ],
    },

    item_manipulation: {
      contract: {
        name: "item_manipulation",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "5838cec2497623cf8e67c6319c72e81ea8571af17e4953227bf38ad2dee0a158",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "grab_item",
          tool: "brain.grab_item",
          description: "Close one of the robot's grippers on an item. gripper_name is one of the names brain.get_state reports for the robot, or empty to let it pick a free gripper suited to the item. Give item_id from brain.identify_item, or leave it empty and give position in the robot frame in meters and, optionally, orientation. max_effort caps the grip in the gripper's effort unit, 0 for the default. Refused when the gripper is unknown or already holds an item, when no gripper is free, or when item_id is one the robot no longer recognizes. Returns the gripper_name and item_id now held; the robot reports it is holding the item from then on.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          // A reach, a close, and a check: several lower level moves in
          // sequence, so the deadline covers the whole sequence.
          deadline_ms: 120000,
        },
        {
          member: "drop_item",
          tool: "brain.drop_item",
          description: "Open one of the robot's grippers where it is and release what it holds. gripper_name is one of the names brain.get_state reports, or empty when exactly one gripper is holding an item. Refused when that gripper holds nothing, or when the name is empty while no gripper or more than one holds. Returns the gripper_name and the item_id released; the robot no longer reports holding it. To carry the item somewhere first, use brain.place_item.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "place_item",
          tool: "brain.place_item",
          description: "Carry the item one of the robot's grippers holds to a pose in the robot frame and release it there. gripper_name as for brain.drop_item. position is where the gripper's grasp point sits when the jaws open, in meters; it is the release point, not a waypoint. orientation is optional; leave it out to let the robot choose. Refused when the gripper holds nothing or the target is unreachable, which the robot refuses rather than approximates. Returns the gripper_name, the item_id placed, and the measured final_position.",
          operation: "long_running",
          safety_sensitive: true,
          // A carry and a release: the same several-move budget as grab_item.
          deadline_ms: 120000,
        },
        {
          member: "abort",
          tool: "brain.abort",
          description: "Stop whatever sequence the robot's brain is running and leave the robot where it stopped, still holding whatever it held. reason is recorded by the robot; empty is accepted. Succeeds even when nothing was running, reporting an empty aborted_action. This is distinct from cancelling the running call or task: any caller may abort, not only the one that started the sequence.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_state",
          tool: "brain.get_state",
          description: "Report what the robot's brain is doing right now. current_action is the running sequence's name, empty when idle; current_action_elapsed_s is how long it has run. gripper_names lists the robot's grippers in a fixed order, the names the other brain tools accept, and holding and held_item_ids are index-aligned with it: true and the item_id where that gripper holds an item, false and empty otherwise.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 2000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // A robot's recorder, where it has one: it films the robot's cameras
    // and limbs into a dataset, an episode at a time.
    recorder: {
      contract: {
        name: "episode_recording",
        tag: "v1",
        sha256: "d646ad5c6e9d18d2db516d7a801b086c37819be14006512ef0081a3b7012f66b",
      },
      actions: [
        {
          member: "record_episode",
          tool: "recorder.record_episode",
          description: "Record one episode of the robot into its open dataset session, under task as the episode's task description; feedback reports frames_recorded and disk_free_bytes as it runs, and cancelling the task ends the episode and saves it. Returns episode_index, frames_recorded and whether the episode was discarded.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: recording accompanies motion.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          // The task parks in `input_required` until the client confirms
          // through `tasks/update`. No goal is sent before that, and only a
          // client with the tasks extension can call the tool.
          confirmation_required: true,
          deadline_ms: 900000,
        },
      ],
      // `finish_session` and `resume_session` are live on the recorder and
      // absent here: the operator opens and closes a dataset session, a
      // model records episodes into it.
    },
  },
}
